That was your misstep. What you wanted to do was get djgpp and install
it. gcc *will* work on MS windows by itself, but you'll have to modify
your autoexec.bat file to do it. With djgpp, you get the compiler, the
libraries and editor, man and the basic unix utilities. Or in other
words, a micro version of linux. There's even emacs. It will compile
nethack (in fact that's the recommended route). 

Give it a try:

http://www.delorie.com/djgpp/

Once you get the zip files, you unzip them with paths to one directory,
make a few minor modifications to your autoexec.bat file, reboot, and
READ THE FAQ. 

As for tutorials, get yourself a copy of "The C Programming Language" by
Dennis Kernighan and Brian Ritchie. You should be able to find it at any
bookstore that sells computer books. 

